","text":"spreads as a percentage of the respective values in the Leland (1994) model. However; in contrast to Figure 3, that corresponds to a different value of the grace period d, is computed based on different optimal value of the coupon rate c For consistency; we select both VB and C to maximize firm value. point every"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Two of our main conclusions with respect to the suboptimal coupon rate still hold. First, if Chapter 11 is available, a firm stops diluting equity before the equity value reaches zero and files for bankruptcy. Second, firm value increases relative to the Leland model because the Chapter 11 proceedings are less costly than outright liquidation.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":147.00701904296875,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":63.666666666666664,"r":425.6666666666667,"t":207.67368570963538},"charspan":[0,345],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":"Two of our main conclusions with respect to the suboptimal coupon rate still hold. First, if Chapter 11 is available, a firm stops diluting equity before the equity value reaches zero and files for bankruptcy. Second, firm value increases relative to the Leland model because the Chapter 11 proceedings are less costly than outright liquidation."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"However; there is a difference between this case and the case in which the coupon is assumed to be the same for all parameters: While the bankruptcy boundary for the case 0 509 and @ = 09 is the highest, it is one of the lower ones in Figure 3. This arises because the typical value of the optimal coupon","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":98.67368570963538,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.0,"r":426.3333333333333,"t":148.34035237630212},"charspan":[0,304],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/43","text":"However; there is a difference between this case and the case in which the coupon is assumed to be the same